In this lesson, we'll look at percentage increase and decrease, and the trick is to turn the percentage into a multiplier first, then just multiply. For an increase, you keep all of the original, which is one hundred percent, and then add the extra percentage on top. So if something goes up by twenty percent, your multiplier is one point two, and you multiply the original by that. For a decrease, you take away instead of add, so a fifteen percent decrease gives you a multiplier of zero point eight five. The original just gets smaller by that fraction. Now, here's something handy. If you've already got the new value and you want to find the original, you simply reverse it by dividing by the same multiplier. So to undo an increase, you divide by one plus p over one hundred, and to undo a decrease, you divide by one minus p over one hundred. One quick warning though. A fifty percent increase followed by a fifty percent decrease does not get you back to where you started, because the second change is working off the bigger number, not the original. Watch out for that one. Now let's see it in action.